        <fr:title text="Issue">Issue</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>
        Each <html:code>C-c C-l</html:code>, <html:code>C-c C-,</html:code> takes so long, it's difficult to do
        focused work.
        I delineate "fast enough" from "too slow" when I find
        myself either
        <html:ul><html:li>
                Finding other low-attention work to do on the computer-- like
                filling out forms-- while I'm waiting for emacs to color the agda
                buffer and stop saying 'Agda is busy with something'
            </html:li>
            <html:li>
                Doing literally anything else on the computer, which usually ends up being r*dd*t, much to the detriment of my productivity
            </html:li></html:ul></html:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <html:p><html:p>
        Somewhat in order
    </html:p>
    <html:ul><html:li><fr:link href="https://agda.readthedocs.io/en/latest/tools/performance.html" type="external">Interactive syntax highlighting</fr:link></html:li>
        <html:li>
            Enable <html:code>{-# OPTIONS --lossy-unification -}</html:code> at the top of the current agda file
        </html:li>
        <html:li>
            Comment out (especially slow) parts of the current agda file that you don't strictly need to work on this hole
            This is usually the easiest, most effective hammer.
        </html:li>
        <html:li>
            Use opaque.
            Something simple but I only just realized after a lot of head banging:
            If you use <html:code>_</html:code> in a type, opaque can't export the type. You
            have to <html:code>C-c C-s</html:code> the hole <html:em>first</html:em>, and export the
            potentially very ugly proof term.
            This is typically used to make expensive path proofs opaque.
            The types of the path need to be non-opaque to be useful.
        </html:li>
        <html:li>
            Build Agda with the cabal flag <html:code>cabal configure -f
            optimise-heavily</html:code>, and maybe try the cabal.project(.local) option
            <html:code>optimization: 2</html:code>? Newer versions of Agda may not be able to infer all of the same
            <html:code>_</html:code> holes, so maybe checkout <html:code>v2.7.0.1</html:code> or what have you.
            This kind of works? TODO: Benchmark?
        </html:li>
        <html:li>
            I haven't been able to get any positive result by tinkering with
            the ghc runtime settings <html:code>+RTS -RTS</html:code>.
            About <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {1}{4}]]></fr:tex> of the time is spent in GC.
            I tried to offload some of that work to another cpu, but was not successful in my attempts.
        </html:li></html:ul></html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
